use /lib
use /ui
from /scope/planning import Planning

export ui-component RibbonPlanningTab:
    pitch:
        The ribbon's Planning tab: the commands for planning work before it is
        sent, as the @{Planning} says.

    description:
        The Planning tab of the ribbon, between Project and Code. It takes no
        mode's colour: its label is in the tab row's own colour, as Project's
        and Application's are.

        Opening it, by clicking it, with <Alt+2>, or by Ctrl+clicking it
        (Cmd+click on macOS) to open it alongside other tabs, as the
        ribbon's keyboard and opening several tabs say, opens Planning's tab
        in the body and selects it, or selects it if it is already open,
        exactly as Open Planning does, by the same route. Ctrl+clicking it
        while it is already open closes only the ribbon tab, and opens
        nothing. Switching the ribbon from Planning to another tab, or
        closing Planning among several, leaves the body as it is: Planning's
        tab stays in the body's tab bar, and the body's selected tab stays
        selected.

    groups:
        A "Plan" group holds the Open Planning command, then New Story, then
        Send Sprint. An "Agent" group follows it, holding Draft Stories, then
        Research.

    commands:
        openPlanning:
            A "Planning" button with a checklist icon. Clicking it opens the
            Planning tab in the body, or selects it where it is open. Its
            tooltip says it opens the project's stories and sprint.

            It is primary, and a full button.

        newStory:
            A "New Story" button with a plus icon. Clicking it opens Planning
            and adds a story to Next sprint, its title focused.

            It is primary, and a slim button.

        sendSprint:
            A "Send Sprint" button with a send icon, enabled while Next
            sprint holds a story not yet sent. Clicking it sends the sprint,
            as Planning's Send sprint does; its tooltip says how many stories
            it sends.

            It is a slim button, and not primary.

        draftStories:
            A "Draft Stories" button with a sparkle icon. Clicking it opens
            Planning, or selects its tab where it is open, and opens drafting
            in the side panel with its "Describe what you want" box focused,
            exactly as the Backlog heading's "Draft stories" button and
            Ctrl+Shift+D do, by the same route, not a copy of it. Its tooltip
            says it asks the agent to break a request into proposed stories,
            and gives Ctrl+Shift+D (Cmd+Shift+D on macOS). It is never
            disabled while planning is paused, since drafting only changes
            the plan.

            It is a slim button, and not primary.

        research:
            A "Research" button with a magnifying-glass icon. Clicking it
            opens Planning, or selects its tab where it is open, and opens
            research in the side panel with its "What should be looked
            into?" box focused, exactly as the Backlog heading's "Research"
            button and Ctrl+Shift+R do, by the same route, not a copy of it.
            Its tooltip says it asks the agent to look into a topic, read
            only, and gives Ctrl+Shift+R (Cmd+Shift+R on macOS). It is never
            disabled while planning is paused; a second research while paused
            keeps the pause, as Planning says.

            It is a slim button, and not primary.
